\f0\fs32 \cf0 Researchers have long debated the relationship between brain size and intelligence. Earlier explorations of this relationship were plagued by crude measurements (weighing brains after death). In 1991, Willeman et al. conducted a study that used magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) to measure brain size. The MRI scans consisted of 18 horizontal MR images that were 5 mm thick and 2.5 mm apart. Further, each image covered a 256 \'d7 256 pixel area. Any pixel with a non-zero gray scale was considered to be \'93part of the brain\'94. The data set contains
\fs24  
\fs32 the study data for 38 subjects including\
\uc0\u8232 MRICount = total pixel count with non-zero gray scale in the 18 MRI scans
\fs24  
\fs32 (the larger the count, the larger the brain!) \
Height = height of the subject (in inches)\
Weight = weight of the subject (in pounds)
\fs24 \

\fs32 VIQ = verbal IQ score\
PIQ = performance IQ score \
FSIQ = full-scale IQ\
Gender = gender of the subject\
\
Source: EESEE
